<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spanish Inquisition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1478">1478</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Tribunal of the Holy Office of the Inquisition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> was a church tribunal under royal control, authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> in 1478 at the request of Isabella I and Ferdinand II. Its first targets were converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> suspected of secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>; it later pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, Protestants and other dissenters across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and its empire. Under inquisitors such as Tomás de Torquemada it held public autos-da-fé, and it was finally abolished in 1834.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition.jpg" alt="Spanish Inquisition" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Di (they-them) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inquisici%C3%B3n_espa%C3%B1ola.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>During a stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1477 and 1478 Queen Isabella I became convinced that Andalusian conversos were secretly practising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. A report by Pedro González de Mendoza, Archbishop of Seville, and the Dominican Tomás de Torquemada, confessor to the monarchs, supported this. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> granted the bull Exigit sincerae devotionis affectus in 1478, allowing the monarchs to appoint three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> or priests as inquisitors, and the first inquisitors, Miguel de Morillo and Juan de San Martín, were named in 1480. Because the crown chose them, the tribunal replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medieval-inquisition">Medieval Inquisition</a>, which had been under papal control, and became an instrument of state power.</p><p>Its attention spread from conversos from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, converts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, after the decrees of 1492 and 1502 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Muslims to convert, leave or face death. It also pursued Alumbrados, people labelled Lutheran, and those accused of witchcraft, blasphemy, bigamy and Freemasonry, and it worked in Southern Italy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> as well as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The crown appointed the Inquisitor General, whose authority reached across the whole empire, and he presided over the Council of the Supreme and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, created in 1483. Below it sat local tribunals, at first itinerant and later fixed, such as those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1482 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1485. Each had two inquisitors, theological assessors, a bailiff and a prosecutor, and in 1608 Philip III required inquisitors to have a legal background.</p><p>Torquemada set out the procedure in a 28-article code in 1484 that stayed largely unchanged for over three centuries. On arriving in a city an inquisitor read an Edict of Grace, which allowed people thirty to forty days to confess and denounce others without harsh penalties. After about 1500 the Edicts of Faith dropped the grace period. Accusers' names were kept from the accused, who was assigned a defence counsel. Sentences ranged from acquittal, which was rare, to penance, exile, flogging, service as a galley oarsman or handing over to the civil authorities for execution. The sentence was announced at an auto de fe, which grew into an elaborate public ceremony. The last public auto took place in 1691.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians estimate that about 150,000 people were prosecuted and that 3,000 to 5,000 were executed, mostly early in its history. Property confiscation was an important aspect of many punishments, and contemporaries said that the rich were burnt for their money. Blood purity statutes, first enacted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1449, barred conversos from offices and were used by many bodies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> also issued Indexes of prohibited books, the first in 1551. Its activity declined during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and how severe it really was remains a subject of dispute among historians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spanish Inquisition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anti-Jewish riots in 1391 forced mass conversions, and suspicion of the converts, or conversos, later fed the <b>Inquisition</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious uniformity was enforced by the <b>Spanish Inquisition</b> and by statutes of ‘purity of blood’ that discriminated against descendants of converted Jews and Muslims.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1412 the kingdom belonged to the Crown of Aragon and later to the Spanish monarchy, ruled by viceroys in Palermo, with Spanish councils and the <b>Inquisition</b>, introduced in 1487, but it kept its own laws, parliament and privileges.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Crown held extensive rights over the Church under the royal patronage, and the <b>Inquisition</b> established a tribunal in Mexico City in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
